&ldquo;people&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural noun &ldquo;stories,&rdquo; not the plural noun &ldquo;peoples&rdquo; and the singular possessive noun &ldquo;story&rsquo;s.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the context requires the plural possessive noun &ldquo;people&rsquo;s,&rdquo; not the plural noun &ldquo;peoples.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun &ldquo;stories,&rdquo; not the singular possessive noun &ldquo;story&rsquo;s.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-703-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"2e6cf74d-7f29-4d3f-95b5-543016fa59ea","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959347,"updateDate":1691007959347}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959347, 1691007959347),
    ($SATQ$166efaa2$SATQ$, $SATQ$de4bc4b7-6302-45b9-b7f9-3ab13226a04a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b19f616c-2543-43ab-b08f-4a2f9911a4a9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Public-awareness campaigns about the need to reduce single-use plastics can be successful, says researcher Kim Borg of Monash University in Australia, when these campaigns give consumers a choice: for example, Japan achieved a 40 percent reduction in plastic-bag use after cashiers were instructed to ask customers whether <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> wanted a bag.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f4d3a25e-04bd-4165-9d5e-c653acae83e3","content":"<p>they</p>"},{"id":"a31c334a-b07d-4de6-ad08-fcef25b4a519","content":"<p>one</p>"},{"id":"bef7c81d-62bf-4139-a646-7dfc9fa86b69","content":"<p>you</p>"},{"id":"83d462eb-8c13-4997-85a2-a820a59bc3c8","content":"<p>it</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f4d3a25e-04bd-4165-9d5e-c653acae83e3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is pronoun&ndash;antecedent agreement. The plural pronoun &ldquo;they&rdquo; agrees in number with the plural antecedent &ldquo;customers.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the singular pronoun &ldquo;one&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural antecedent &ldquo;customers.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the second person pronoun &ldquo;you&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t conventional as a substitute for &ldquo;customers.&rdquo; It suggests that the audience (&ldquo;you&rdquo;) is the customer. Choice D is incorrect because the singular pronoun &ldquo;it&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural antecedent &ldquo;customers.&rdquo; </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-636-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$cade368a-e612-4051-93b6-1760a0eb4985$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"166efaa2","external_id":"de4bc4b7-6302-45b9-b7f9-3ab13226a04a","vaultid":"b19f616c-2543-43ab-b08f-4a2f9911a4a9","uId":"30fc84b5-20ae-4d7d-a5be-8b7cb1c66e3f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Public-awareness campaigns about the need to reduce single-use plastics can be successful, says researcher Kim Borg of Monash University in Australia, when these campaigns give consumers a choice: for example, Japan achieved a 40 percent reduction in plastic-bag use after cashiers were instructed to ask customers whether <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> wanted a bag.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f4d3a25e-04bd-4165-9d5e-c653acae83e3","content":"<p>they</p>"},{"id":"a31c334a-b07d-4de6-ad08-fcef25b4a519","content":"<p>one</p>"},{"id":"bef7c81d-62bf-4139-a646-7dfc9fa86b69","content":"<p>you</p>"},{"id":"83d462eb-8c13-4997-85a2-a820a59bc3c8","content":"<p>it</p>"}],"keys":["f4d3a25e-04bd-4165-9d5e-c653acae83e3"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is pronoun&ndash;antecedent agreement. The plural pronoun &ldquo;they&rdquo; agrees in number with the plural antecedent &ldquo;customers.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the singular pronoun &ldquo;one&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural antecedent &ldquo;customers.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the second person pronoun &ldquo;you&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t conventional as a substitute for &ldquo;customers.&rdquo; It suggests that the audience (&ldquo;you&rdquo;) is the customer. Choice D is incorrect because the singular pronoun &ldquo;it&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural antecedent &ldquo;customers.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-636-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"cade368a-e612-4051-93b6-1760a0eb4985","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959340,"updateDate":1691007959340}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959340, 1691007959340),
    ($SATQ$1684b237$SATQ$, $SATQ$1827c508-38a4-4882-9007-2e81c2a8cd8a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>One of the few African American global explorers during the turn of the 20th century, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d5be6ef7-2168-49fd-839f-0978e10c46dc","content":"<p>Matthew Henson made several treks across Greenland between 1891 and 1909.</p>"},{"id":"c364b71a-3d61-47c7-a1b9-9df1815205a0","content":"<p>1891 and 1909 were the years between which Matthew Henson made several treks across Greenland.</p>"},{"id":"4dbc8200-81fc-4ec3-92b4-abc8e5d4a58a","content":"<p>Greenland was where Matthew Henson made several treks between 1891 and 1909.</p>"},{"id":"2aa0825a-0f7e-4d79-8b74-38cbb6a3d555","content":"<p>several treks across Greenland were made by Matthew Henson between 1891 and 1909.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d5be6ef7-2168-49fd-839f-0978e10c46dc"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the proper noun "Matthew Henson" the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase "one&hellip;century." In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that Matthew Henson&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;is being described as one of the few African American global explorers during the turn of the 20th century.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase "1891 and 1909" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that those years were one of the few African American global explorers during the turn of the 20th century. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the proper noun "Greenland" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that Greenland was one of the few African American global explorers during the turn of the 20th century. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase "several treks across Greenland" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the treks were one of the few African American global explorers during the turn of the 20th century.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1684b237","external_id":"1827c508-38a4-4882-9007-2e81c2a8cd8a","vaultid":null,"uId":"4c9e08d1-0b33-4578-87ce-d400cbfa5b90","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>One of the few African American global explorers during the turn of the 20th century, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d5be6ef7-2168-49fd-839f-0978e10c46dc","content":"<p>Matthew Henson made several treks across Greenland between 1891 and 1909.</p>"},{"id":"c364b71a-3d61-47c7-a1b9-9df1815205a0","content":"<p>1891 and 1909 were the years between which Matthew Henson made several treks across Greenland.</p>"},{"id":"4dbc8200-81fc-4ec3-92b4-abc8e5d4a58a","content":"<p>Greenland was where Matthew Henson made several treks between 1891 and 1909.</p>"},{"id":"2aa0825a-0f7e-4d79-8b74-38cbb6a3d555","content":"<p>several treks across Greenland were made by Matthew Henson between 1891 and 1909.</p>"}],"keys":["d5be6ef7-2168-49fd-839f-0978e10c46dc"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the proper noun \"Matthew Henson\" the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase \"one&hellip;century.\" In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that Matthew Henson&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;is being described as one of the few African American global explorers during the turn of the 20th century.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase \"1891 and 1909\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that those years were one of the few African American global explorers during the turn of the 20th century. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the proper noun \"Greenland\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that Greenland was one of the few African American global explorers during the turn of the 20th century. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase \"several treks across Greenland\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the treks were one of the few African American global explorers during the turn of the 20th century.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323047,"updateDate":1730147323047}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323047, 1730147323047),
    ($SATQ$1724dac2$SATQ$, $SATQ$86cf8f91-b7a8-4855-8d80-1cbaa66e7529$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$1c04ced8-0978-4ab8-8ffe-eb9f3519a855$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A subseasonal weather forecast attempts to predict weather conditions three to four weeks in <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> its predictions are therefore more short-term than those of the seasonal forecast, which attempts to predict the weather more than a month in advance.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"14f26290-763e-4bbb-abd3-d5f6197f3eb8","content":"<p>advance,</p>"},{"id":"8b0a764f-425e-4da7-92b4-b436b3901f98","content":"<p>advance</p>"},{"id":"56d2c28f-6b91-495f-a534-e89fd9b9312c","content":"<p>advance;</p>"},{"id":"f57794c3-9f62-44b0-9183-48ab1e81300e","content":"<p>advance and</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["56d2c28f-6b91-495f-a534-e89fd9b9312c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The clause &ldquo;A subseasonal&hellip;advance&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;its predictions&hellip;forecast&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so using a semicolon to separate them is grammatically correct.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. The clause &ldquo;A subseasonal&hellip;advance&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;its predictions&hellip;forecast&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so a comma is not enough to separate them.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. The clause &ldquo;A subseasonal&hellip;advance&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;its predictions&hellip;forecast&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so they need to be separated with specific punctuation (a period, a semi-colon, a colon, a dash, or a comma + a coordinating conjunction).&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. The clause &ldquo;A subseasonal&hellip;advance&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;its predictions&hellip;forecast&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so the word &ldquo;and&rdquo; by itself is not enough to separate them. There would need to be a comma before &ldquo;and&rdquo; for this choice to work.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-M002$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$7ae798be-757c-4a94-bb74-2da299a1a28c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1724dac2","external_id":"86cf8f91-b7a8-4855-8d80-1cbaa66e7529","vaultid":"1c04ced8-0978-4ab8-8ffe-eb9f3519a855","uId":"36fed738-e50d-49ac-8334-5bdefe3e7f93","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A subseasonal weather forecast attempts to predict weather conditions three to four weeks in <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> its predictions are therefore more short-term than those of the seasonal forecast, which attempts to predict the weather more than a month in advance.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"14f26290-763e-4bbb-abd3-d5f6197f3eb8","content":"<p>advance,</p>"},{"id":"8b0a764f-425e-4da7-92b4-b436b3901f98","content":"<p>advance</p>"},{"id":"56d2c28f-6b91-495f-a534-e89fd9b9312c","content":"<p>advance;</p>"},{"id":"f57794c3-9f62-44b0-9183-48ab1e81300e","content":"<p>advance and</p>"}],"keys":["56d2c28f-6b91-495f-a534-e89fd9b9312c"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The clause &ldquo;A subseasonal&hellip;advance&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;its predictions&hellip;forecast&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so using a semicolon to separate them is grammatically correct.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. The clause &ldquo;A subseasonal&hellip;advance&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;its predictions&hellip;forecast&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so a comma is not enough to separate them.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. The clause &ldquo;A subseasonal&hellip;advance&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;its predictions&hellip;forecast&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so they need to be separated with specific punctuation (a period, a semi-colon, a colon, a dash, or a comma + a coordinating conjunction).&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. The clause &ldquo;A subseasonal&hellip;advance&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;its predictions&hellip;forecast&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so the word &ldquo;and&rdquo; by itself is not enough to separate them. There would need to be a comma before &ldquo;and&rdquo; for this choice to work.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-M002","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7ae798be-757c-4a94-bb74-2da299a1a28c","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959359,"updateDate":1691007959359}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959359, 1691007959359),
    ($SATQ$175df826$SATQ$, $SATQ$1ec934c9-2fc7-4872-ad4a-c25c8e18bbce$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$950ae2c4-689a-47e1-b8a3-fcdf9bc6795b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In the 2011 documentary <em>The Barber of Birmingham</em>, civil rights activist James Armstrong recounts how his barbershop in Birmingham, Alabama, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> as a political hub for members of the Black community during the 1950s.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4501b263-0e62-4bb3-acb4-54a7560547be","content":"<p>serving</p>"},{"id":"51ef56e0-e896-4475-b760-01285f5e66b5","content":"<p>having served</p>"},{"id":"34a92278-8af8-49ee-8b0b-4d013b51058c","content":"<p>served</p>"},{"id":"95e5f0e9-cf76-4f5b-8e95-7409b9b64bd3","content":"<p>to serve</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["34a92278-8af8-49ee-8b0b-4d013b51058c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. Relative clauses, such as the one beginning with "how," require a finite (tensed) verb, a verb that can function as the main verb of a clause. This choice correctly supplies the clause with the finite past tense verb "served."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle "serving" doesn&rsquo;t supply the clause with a finite verb. Choice B is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle "having served" doesn&rsquo;t supply the clause with a finite verb. Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive "to serve" doesn&rsquo;t supply the clause with a finite verb.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-717-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$30ea13e3-5665-4dde-9dcf-396cf74359e2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"175df826","external_id":"1ec934c9-2fc7-4872-ad4a-c25c8e18bbce","vaultid":"950ae2c4-689a-47e1-b8a3-fcdf9bc6795b","uId":"a9be64a9-bd2b-4f07-acca-ddab8d5d52ab","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In the 2011 documentary <em>The Barber of Birmingham</em>, civil rights activist James Armstrong recounts how his barbershop in Birmingham, Alabama, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> as a political hub for members of the Black community during the 1950s.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4501b263-0e62-4bb3-acb4-54a7560547be","content":"<p>serving</p>"},{"id":"51ef56e0-e896-4475-b760-01285f5e66b5","content":"<p>having served</p>"},{"id":"34a92278-8af8-49ee-8b0b-4d013b51058c","content":"<p>served</p>"},{"id":"95e5f0e9-cf76-4f5b-8e95-7409b9b64bd3","content":"<p>to serve</p>"}],"keys":["34a92278-8af8-49ee-8b0b-4d013b51058c"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. Relative clauses, such as the one beginning with \"how,\" require a finite (tensed) verb, a verb that can function as the main verb of a clause. This choice correctly supplies the clause with the finite past tense verb \"served.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle \"serving\" doesn&rsquo;t supply the clause with a finite verb. Choice B is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle \"having served\" doesn&rsquo;t supply the clause with a finite verb. Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive \"to serve\" doesn&rsquo;t supply the clause with a finite verb.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-717-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"30ea13e3-5665-4dde-9dcf-396cf74359e2","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959348,"updateDate":1691007959348}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959348, 1691007959348),
    ($SATQ$18382e67$SATQ$, $SATQ$be69a3ae-b262-4c63-b611-2309cbe6870a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In Marisol&rsquo;s 1968 sculpture <em>Mi Mama y Yo</em>, gone are the types of pop culture references that made the Parisian-born Venezuelan American artist a star at the height of the pop art movement. In <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> place is a far more personal subject: a sculptural depiction of the artist as a young girl with her mother.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a4f50c29-033a-4a98-ac3e-6cf3f4b25623","content":"<p>its</p>"},{"id":"3bcabe9a-0594-4be8-ada5-067ffcf69224","content":"<p>they&rsquo;re</p>"},{"id":"e906b2be-d02c-4da7-a246-b3e65a8d8b3a","content":"<p>their</p>"},{"id":"6ac836e7-854d-447d-92c5-a35541722ebe","content":"<p>it&rsquo;s</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e906b2be-d02c-4da7-a246-b3e65a8d8b3a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of possessive determiners. The plural possessive determiner "their" agrees in number with the plural noun "types" and thus indicates that the more personal subject matter of Marisol&rsquo;s 1968 sculpture takes the place of those types of pop culture references that made Marisol a star.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the singular possessive determiner "its" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural noun "types." Choice B is incorrect because "they&rsquo;re" is the contraction for "they are," not a possessive determiner. Choice D is incorrect because "it&rsquo;s" is the contraction for "it is" or "it has," not a possessive determiner.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"18382e67","external_id":"be69a3ae-b262-4c63-b611-2309cbe6870a","vaultid":null,"uId":"d7dac7c6-033c-4e05-bdb4-d5aa1dfd4069","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In Marisol&rsquo;s 1968 sculpture <em>Mi Mama y Yo</em>, gone are the types of pop culture references that made the Parisian-born Venezuelan American artist a star at the height of the pop art movement. In <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> place is a far more personal subject: a sculptural depiction of the artist as a young girl with her mother.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a4f50c29-033a-4a98-ac3e-6cf3f4b25623","content":"<p>its</p>"},{"id":"3bcabe9a-0594-4be8-ada5-067ffcf69224","content":"<p>they&rsquo;re</p>"},{"id":"e906b2be-d02c-4da7-a246-b3e65a8d8b3a","content":"<p>their</p>"},{"id":"6ac836e7-854d-447d-92c5-a35541722ebe","content":"<p>it&rsquo;s</p>"}],"keys":["e906b2be-d02c-4da7-a246-b3e65a8d8b3a"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of possessive determiners. The plural possessive determiner \"their\" agrees in number with the plural noun \"types\" and thus indicates that the more personal subject matter of Marisol&rsquo;s 1968 sculpture takes the place of those types of pop culture references that made Marisol a star.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the singular possessive determiner \"its\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural noun \"types.\" Choice B is incorrect because \"they&rsquo;re\" is the contraction for \"they are,\" not a possessive determiner. Choice D is incorrect because \"it&rsquo;s\" is the contraction for \"it is\" or \"it has,\" not a possessive determiner.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669213,"updateDate":1743430669213}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669213, 1743430669213),
    ($SATQ$188f7e3c$SATQ$, $SATQ$e941b046-f212-43a5-9e8f-2301a133cef6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2690e87e-ca16-4d34-9efe-bfc1aa20b0d8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 2016, engineer Vanessa Galvez oversaw the installation of 164 bioswales, vegetated channels designed to absorb and divert stormwater, along the streets of Queens, New York. By reducing the runoff flowing into city sewers, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0c67d7c2-d3ff-4cb7-adaf-ce7b210eb324","content":"<p>the mitigation of both street flooding and the resulting pollution of nearby waterways has been achieved by bioswales.</p>"},{"id":"145fa42a-c8eb-49ce-922b-09d079ac2da5","content":"<p>the bioswales have mitigated both street flooding and the resulting pollution of nearby waterways.</p>"},{"id":"b2980b7f-3604-413d-a94e-730cd978179c","content":"<p>the bioswales&rsquo; mitigation of both street flooding and the resulting pollution of nearby waterways has been achieved.</p>"},{"id":"3f814773-30b1-4967-99da-40161e793641","content":"<p>both street flooding and the resulting pollution of nearby waterways have been mitigated by bioswales.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["145fa42a-c8eb-49ce-922b-09d079ac2da5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase &ldquo;the bioswales&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;By reducing&hellip;sewers.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that the bioswales&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;are reducing runoff flowing into city sewers. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the mitigation&hellip;waterways&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase results in unclear modification. The resulting sentence makes it hard to determine what is responsible for &ldquo;reducing the runoff&rdquo;: the bioswales or some other noun in the sentence. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the bioswales&rsquo; mitigation&hellip;waterways&rdquo; &nbsp;immediately after the modifying phrase results in unclear modification. The resulting sentence makes it hard to determine what is responsible for &ldquo;reducing the runoff&rdquo;: the bioswales or some other noun in the sentence. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;street flooding and the resulting pollution&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the &ldquo;flooding and pollution&rdquo; are reducing runoff flowing into city sewers. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-645-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$bced425e-44f1-46c5-9396-fced022f6e3f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"188f7e3c","external_id":"e941b046-f212-43a5-9e8f-2301a133cef6","vaultid":"2690e87e-ca16-4d34-9efe-bfc1aa20b0d8","uId":"8e5c179b-d044-49e8-899d-a706619ea687","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 2016, engineer Vanessa Galvez oversaw the installation of 164 bioswales, vegetated channels designed to absorb and divert stormwater, along the streets of Queens, New York. By reducing the runoff flowing into city sewers, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0c67d7c2-d3ff-4cb7-adaf-ce7b210eb324","content":"<p>the mitigation of both street flooding and the resulting pollution of nearby waterways has been achieved by bioswales.</p>"},{"id":"145fa42a-c8eb-49ce-922b-09d079ac2da5","content":"<p>the bioswales have mitigated both street flooding and the resulting pollution of nearby waterways.</p>"},{"id":"b2980b7f-3604-413d-a94e-730cd978179c","content":"<p>the bioswales&rsquo; mitigation of both street flooding and the resulting pollution of nearby waterways has been achieved.</p>"},{"id":"3f814773-30b1-4967-99da-40161e793641","content":"<p>both street flooding and the resulting pollution of nearby waterways have been mitigated by bioswales.</p>"}],"keys":["145fa42a-c8eb-49ce-922b-09d079ac2da5"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase &ldquo;the bioswales&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;By reducing&hellip;sewers.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that the bioswales&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;are reducing runoff flowing into city sewers. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the mitigation&hellip;waterways&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase results in unclear modification. The resulting sentence makes it hard to determine what is responsible for &ldquo;reducing the runoff&rdquo;: the bioswales or some other noun in the sentence. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the bioswales&rsquo; mitigation&hellip;waterways&rdquo; &nbsp;immediately after the modifying phrase results in unclear modification. The resulting sentence makes it hard to determine what is responsible for &ldquo;reducing the runoff&rdquo;: the bioswales or some other noun in the sentence. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;street flooding and the resulting pollution&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the &ldquo;flooding and pollution&rdquo; are reducing runoff flowing into city sewers. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-645-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"bced425e-44f1-46c5-9396-fced022f6e3f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959340,"updateDate":1691007959340}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959340, 1691007959340),
    ($SATQ$1a61e2ae$SATQ$, $SATQ$a7dc218f-7af4-4a81-8a3e-dc36da41b8e2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>English poet and Shakespeare contemporary John Donne&rsquo;s <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> much admired during his lifetime (1572&ndash;1631) and in the decades that followed, had, at the time of their enthusiastic rediscovery by the early twentieth-century modernists, been essentially gathering dust for the intervening 250 years.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bddfd4f1-1c0d-426c-ac9e-8b241887eadb","content":"<p>works were</p>"},{"id":"7e08abbd-d858-42a3-bc23-e13ca6307d55","content":"<p>works<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> were</p>"},{"id":"ebc20a91-3797-4781-914d-f10ad285cd6d","content":"<p>works<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span>&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"0c8d69ba-e7c6-477b-88f7-36d6f6866303","content":"<p>works had been</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ebc20a91-3797-4781-914d-f10ad285cd6d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation and verb forms within a sentence. This choice leaves the verb "admired" in its nonfinite past participle form to function within a supplementary element ("much&hellip;followed"). Offset by commas after "works" and "followed," this supplementary element interrupts the main clause ("English poet and Shakespeare contemporary John Donne&rsquo;s works&hellip;had&hellip;been essentially gathering dust&hellip;") with additional information about the works&rsquo; reception during Donne&rsquo;s lifetime.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to offset the supplementary element ("much&hellip;followed") with appropriate punctuation, and using the finite verb "were much admired" results in an ungrammatical sentence. Choice B is incorrect because using the finite verb "were much admired" results in an ungrammatical sentence. Choice D is incorrect because it fails to offset the supplementary element ("much&hellip;followed") with appropriate punctuation, and using the finite verb "had been much admired" results in an ungrammatical sentence.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1a61e2ae","external_id":"a7dc218f-7af4-4a81-8a3e-dc36da41b8e2","vaultid":null,"uId":"42e8495a-0bc6-4dcc-992c-64256ddff962","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>English poet and Shakespeare contemporary John Donne&rsquo;s <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> much admired during his lifetime (1572&ndash;1631) and in the decades that followed, had, at the time of their enthusiastic rediscovery by the early twentieth-century modernists, been essentially gathering dust for the intervening 250 years.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"bddfd4f1-1c0d-426c-ac9e-8b241887eadb","content":"<p>works were</p>"},{"id":"7e08abbd-d858-42a3-bc23-e13ca6307d55","content":"<p>works<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> were</p>"},{"id":"ebc20a91-3797-4781-914d-f10ad285cd6d","content":"<p>works<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span>&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"0c8d69ba-e7c6-477b-88f7-36d6f6866303","content":"<p>works had been</p>"}],"keys":["ebc20a91-3797-4781-914d-f10ad285cd6d"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation and verb forms within a sentence. This choice leaves the verb \"admired\" in its nonfinite past participle form to function within a supplementary element (\"much&hellip;followed\"). Offset by commas after \"works\" and \"followed,\" this supplementary element interrupts the main clause (\"English poet and Shakespeare contemporary John Donne&rsquo;s works&hellip;had&hellip;been essentially gathering dust&hellip;\") with additional information about the works&rsquo; reception during Donne&rsquo;s lifetime.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to offset the supplementary element (\"much&hellip;followed\") with appropriate punctuation, and using the finite verb \"were much admired\" results in an ungrammatical sentence. Choice B is incorrect because using the finite verb \"were much admired\" results in an ungrammatical sentence. Choice D is incorrect because it fails to offset the supplementary element (\"much&hellip;followed\") with appropriate punctuation, and using the finite verb \"had been much admired\" results in an ungrammatical sentence.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669212,"updateDate":1743430669212}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669212, 1743430669212),
    ($SATQ$1aa3f174$SATQ$, $SATQ$f6c312e1-2810-41e4-b1c2-6e68694d5b44$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The Mesoamerican city of Teotihuacan featured a uniquely egalitarian urban housing infrastructure. Built between the first and seventh centuries CE, Teotihuacan housed its residents (as many as 200,000, by some <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in a complex of comfortable apartments of comparable size.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3aefc259-6793-4f91-9ad6-86d98ab7ae74","content":"<p>estimates<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">)</span></p>"},{"id":"f21e2909-4a28-4c55-804f-580ed15d5720","content":"<p>estimates<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">),</span></p>"},{"id":"b81edf95-1784-472b-879d-41454e50dbd8","content":"<p>estimates<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">&mdash;</span></p>"},{"id":"35951b64-1447-4138-a718-f4da69fe5997","content":"<p>estimates</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3aefc259-6793-4f91-9ad6-86d98ab7ae74"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice correctly completes the parenthetical element "as many as 200,000, by some estimates" with a closing parenthesis, pairing with the opening parenthesis that appears earlier in the sentence. This parenthetical element functions to specify the number of residents, and the use of parentheses indicates that this element could be removed without affecting the grammatical coherence of the sentence.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While this choice completes the parenthetical element with a closing parenthesis, no comma is needed before the prepositional phrases ("in...size") that provide essential information about where the residents were housed. Choice C is incorrect because a dash can&rsquo;t be paired with an opening parenthesis in this way to separate the parenthetical element from the rest of the sentence. Choice D is incorrect because it doesn&rsquo;t close the parenthetical element that was opened earlier in the sentence.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1aa3f174","external_id":"f6c312e1-2810-41e4-b1c2-6e68694d5b44","vaultid":null,"uId":"3a4b1b61-5fee-4662-b6a6-e3e963931bec","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The Mesoamerican city of Teotihuacan featured a uniquely egalitarian urban housing infrastructure. Built between the first and seventh centuries CE, Teotihuacan housed its residents (as many as 200,000, by some <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in a complex of comfortable apartments of comparable size.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3aefc259-6793-4f91-9ad6-86d98ab7ae74","content":"<p>estimates<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">)</span></p>"},{"id":"f21e2909-4a28-4c55-804f-580ed15d5720","content":"<p>estimates<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">),</span></p>"},{"id":"b81edf95-1784-472b-879d-41454e50dbd8","content":"<p>estimates<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">&mdash;</span></p>"},{"id":"35951b64-1447-4138-a718-f4da69fe5997","content":"<p>estimates</p>"}],"keys":["3aefc259-6793-4f91-9ad6-86d98ab7ae74"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice correctly completes the parenthetical element \"as many as 200,000, by some estimates\" with a closing parenthesis, pairing with the opening parenthesis that appears earlier in the sentence. This parenthetical element functions to specify the number of residents, and the use of parentheses indicates that this element could be removed without affecting the grammatical coherence of the sentence.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While this choice completes the parenthetical element with a closing parenthesis, no comma is needed before the prepositional phrases (\"in...size\") that provide essential information about where the residents were housed. Choice C is incorrect because a dash can&rsquo;t be paired with an opening parenthesis in this way to separate the parenthetical element from the rest of the sentence. Choice D is incorrect because it doesn&rsquo;t close the parenthetical element that was opened earlier in the sentence.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061193,"updateDate":1755117061193}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061193, 1755117061193),
    ($SATQ$1b97cce9$SATQ$, $SATQ$3e8a570e-28ad-4084-ba82-389eb7175263$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2caee384-7c6d-426b-8f3b-b513c793cadb$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Hegra is an archaeological site in present-day Saudi Arabia and was the second largest city of the Nabataean Kingdom (fourth century BCE to first century CE). Archaeologist Laila Nehm&eacute; recently traveled to Hegra to study its ancient <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> into the rocky outcrops of a vast desert, these burial chambers seem to blend seamlessly with nature.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"eb17bb92-0625-422c-808a-d357189c848c","content":"<p>tombs. Built</p>"},{"id":"0c45e757-d9ff-43da-a584-4f7aa08bd198","content":"<p>tombs, built</p>"},{"id":"c8c75d81-bd51-4f09-90f3-c61125b997a7","content":"<p>tombs and built</p>"},{"id":"a307ba53-bae9-4bbd-9059-937dee316a16","content":"<p>tombs built</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["eb17bb92-0625-422c-808a-d357189c848c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period after &ldquo;tombs&rdquo; is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence (&ldquo;Archaeologist...tombs&rdquo;) and another (&ldquo;Built...nature&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice C is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join the two sentences. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences (&ldquo;Archaeologist...tombs&rdquo; and &ldquo;Built...nature&rdquo;) are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-662-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$244de805-9209-4224-8225-7063f1989ce4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1b97cce9","external_id":"3e8a570e-28ad-4084-ba82-389eb7175263","vaultid":"2caee384-7c6d-426b-8f3b-b513c793cadb","uId":"a10a7e33-a5b1-421a-8c41-342da83e40ca","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Hegra is an archaeological site in present-day Saudi Arabia and was the second largest city of the Nabataean Kingdom (fourth century BCE to first century CE). Archaeologist Laila Nehm&eacute; recently traveled to Hegra to study its ancient <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> into the rocky outcrops of a vast desert, these burial chambers seem to blend seamlessly with nature.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"eb17bb92-0625-422c-808a-d357189c848c","content":"<p>tombs. Built</p>"},{"id":"0c45e757-d9ff-43da-a584-4f7aa08bd198","content":"<p>tombs, built</p>"},{"id":"c8c75d81-bd51-4f09-90f3-c61125b997a7","content":"<p>tombs and built</p>"},{"id":"a307ba53-bae9-4bbd-9059-937dee316a16","content":"<p>tombs built</p>"}],"keys":["eb17bb92-0625-422c-808a-d357189c848c"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period after &ldquo;tombs&rdquo; is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence (&ldquo;Archaeologist...tombs&rdquo;) and another (&ldquo;Built...nature&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice C is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join the two sentences. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences (&ldquo;Archaeologist...tombs&rdquo; and &ldquo;Built...nature&rdquo;) are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-662-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"244de805-9209-4224-8225-7063f1989ce4","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959341,"updateDate":1691007959341}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959341, 1691007959341),
    ($SATQ$1c65db8f$SATQ$, $SATQ$0e4cc810-98b3-497e-b9cd-7415d29d1aef$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A species of <em>Byropsis</em> algae produces toxins to avoid being eaten by predators. However, in some cases, the toxins the organism uses to protect itself from predation actually <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> its attractiveness to predators. The Hawaiian sea slug, for example, not only tolerates <em>Byropsis</em>&nbsp;toxins but actually uses them for protection in the same way the algae does.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7d3a3304-4a03-468c-9b94-83d431fac4bb","content":"<p>is increasing</p>"},{"id":"c4d7dc6c-67fa-426b-8716-66fb51a4591b","content":"<p>increase</p>"},{"id":"9f951aa2-16d0-4dbc-b027-7f05dad9183b","content":"<p>increases</p>"},{"id":"a89c9155-5e76-4851-a491-15f879d46a7b","content":"<p>has increased</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c4d7dc6c-67fa-426b-8716-66fb51a4591b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The plural verb "increase" agrees in number with the plural subject "toxins."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the singular verb "is increasing" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject "toxins." Choice C is incorrect because the singular verb "increases" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject "toxins." Choice D is incorrect because the singular verb "has increased" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject "toxins."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1c65db8f","external_id":"0e4cc810-98b3-497e-b9cd-7415d29d1aef","vaultid":null,"uId":"f299a49d-4ed3-4367-b1bb-2f75f44ce3d9","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A species of <em>Byropsis</em> algae produces toxins to avoid being eaten by predators. However, in some cases, the toxins the organism uses to protect itself from predation actually <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> its attractiveness to predators. The Hawaiian sea slug, for example, not only tolerates <em>Byropsis</em>&nbsp;toxins but actually uses them for protection in the same way the algae does.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7d3a3304-4a03-468c-9b94-83d431fac4bb","content":"<p>is increasing</p>"},{"id":"c4d7dc6c-67fa-426b-8716-66fb51a4591b","content":"<p>increase</p>"},{"id":"9f951aa2-16d0-4dbc-b027-7f05dad9183b","content":"<p>increases</p>"},{"id":"a89c9155-5e76-4851-a491-15f879d46a7b","content":"<p>has increased</p>"}],"keys":["c4d7dc6c-67fa-426b-8716-66fb51a4591b"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The plural verb \"increase\" agrees in number with the plural subject \"toxins.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the singular verb \"is increasing\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject \"toxins.\" Choice C is incorrect because the singular verb \"increases\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject \"toxins.\" Choice D is incorrect because the singular verb \"has increased\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject \"toxins.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323048,"updateDate":1730147323048}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323048, 1730147323048),
    ($SATQ$1d971f75$SATQ$, $SATQ$72facd3e-59dd-45a3-888e-115408f30005$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ac252ce7-0bc9-4ee2-ba84-0b3cf3d5a7ac$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Photographer Ansel Adams&rsquo;s landscape portraits are iconic pieces of American art. However, many of the <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> of landscapes were intended not as art but as marketing; a concessions company at Yosemite National Park had hired Adams to take pictures of the park for restaurant menus and brochures.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7b8bad0b-97a0-4614-8a38-640f4af7097a","content":"<p>photographer&rsquo;s early photo&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"c65392bc-830e-4f54-ab45-c6db6a9ead7a","content":"<p>photographers early photo&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"72fb9aa4-e0d0-4b8d-b8ed-fcf7dacff470","content":"<p>photographer&rsquo;s early photos</p>"},{"id":"d85552ad-1fbf-4b23-b52b-8d97f8a32171","content":"<p>photographers early photos</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["72fb9aa4-e0d0-4b8d-b8ed-fcf7dacff470"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. There&rsquo;s only one photographer (Adams), and the photos are his, so the singular possessive "photographer&rsquo;s" is correct. There&rsquo;s more than one photo, and nothing belongs to the photos, so the simple plural "photos" is correct.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice uses the singular possessive "photo&rsquo;s," which isn&rsquo;t correct. There&rsquo;s more than one photo, and they don&rsquo;t possess anything, so the noun should be the simple plural "photos." Choice B is incorrect. This choice uses the simple plural "photographers" and the singular possessive "photo&rsquo;s," which aren&rsquo;t correct. There&rsquo;s only one photographer (Adams) and there&rsquo;s more than one photo. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses the simple plural "photographers," which isn&rsquo;t correct. There&rsquo;s only one photographer (Adams).</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-FSS-E004$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$05c84c10-bfef-428a-9ae9-b318a4921bb0$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1d971f75","external_id":"72facd3e-59dd-45a3-888e-115408f30005","vaultid":"ac252ce7-0bc9-4ee2-ba84-0b3cf3d5a7ac","uId":"bd49a9c0-7c35-4280-b704-f1044d10add1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Photographer Ansel Adams&rsquo;s landscape portraits are iconic pieces of American art. However, many of the <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> of landscapes were intended not as art but as marketing; a concessions company at Yosemite National Park had hired Adams to take pictures of the park for restaurant menus and brochures.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7b8bad0b-97a0-4614-8a38-640f4af7097a","content":"<p>photographer&rsquo;s early photo&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"c65392bc-830e-4f54-ab45-c6db6a9ead7a","content":"<p>photographers early photo&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"72fb9aa4-e0d0-4b8d-b8ed-fcf7dacff470","content":"<p>photographer&rsquo;s early photos</p>"},{"id":"d85552ad-1fbf-4b23-b52b-8d97f8a32171","content":"<p>photographers early photos</p>"}],"keys":["72fb9aa4-e0d0-4b8d-b8ed-fcf7dacff470"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. There&rsquo;s only one photographer (Adams), and the photos are his, so the singular possessive \"photographer&rsquo;s\" is correct. There&rsquo;s more than one photo, and nothing belongs to the photos, so the simple plural \"photos\" is correct.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice uses the singular possessive \"photo&rsquo;s,\" which isn&rsquo;t correct. There&rsquo;s more than one photo, and they don&rsquo;t possess anything, so the noun should be the simple plural \"photos.\" Choice B is incorrect. This choice uses the simple plural \"photographers\" and the singular possessive \"photo&rsquo;s,\" which aren&rsquo;t correct. There&rsquo;s only one photographer (Adams) and there&rsquo;s more than one photo. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses the simple plural \"photographers,\" which isn&rsquo;t correct. There&rsquo;s only one photographer (Adams).</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-FSS-E004","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"05c84c10-bfef-428a-9ae9-b318a4921bb0","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959366,"updateDate":1691007959366}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959366, 1691007959366),
    ($SATQ$1ee4485c$SATQ$, $SATQ$f60eab77-bd60-4227-a0f7-3a92bc6547e2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$6b7b3fce-b1e3-40ce-87d1-e7f54c575452$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Journalists have dubbed Gil Scott-Heron the &ldquo;godfather of rap,&rdquo; a title that has appeared in hundreds of articles about him since the 1990s. Scott-Heron himself resisted the godfather <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> feeling that it didn&rsquo;t encapsulate his devotion to the broader African American blues music tradition as well as &ldquo;bluesologist,&rdquo; the moniker he preferred. </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"424050e8-2bb1-4738-af57-5b7975bc3677","content":"<p>nickname, however</p>"},{"id":"c575db7e-ec4c-488e-b2c9-9b0298aa29d5","content":"<p>nickname, however;</p>"},{"id":"5325c1ef-cb01-4d45-ae1c-2778a2199f29","content":"<p>nickname, however,</p>"},{"id":"0cf54446-7ec0-4ea8-bb3b-c21b86ad06d6","content":"<p>nickname; however,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5325c1ef-cb01-4d45-ae1c-2778a2199f29"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a main clause and two supplementary elements. In this choice, the commas after &ldquo;nickname&rdquo; and &ldquo;however&rdquo; are correctly used to separate the supplementary adverb &ldquo;however&rdquo; from the main clause (&ldquo;Scott-Heron&hellip;nickname&rdquo;) on one side and the supplementary participial phrase (&ldquo;feeling&hellip;bluesologist&rdquo;) on the other. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the supplementary adverb &ldquo;however&rdquo; and the supplementary phrase (&ldquo;feeling&hellip;bluesologist&rdquo;).&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join the supplementary adverb &ldquo;however&rdquo; and the supplementary phrase (&ldquo;feeling&hellip;bluesologist&rdquo;). Choice D is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join the main clause (&ldquo;Scott-Heron&hellip;nickname&rdquo;) and the supplementary word and phrase (&ldquo;however&rdquo; and &ldquo;feeling&hellip;bluesologist&rdquo;). Moreover, placing the semicolon after &ldquo;nickname&rdquo; illogically signals that the following information (Scott-Heron&rsquo;s feeling that the nickname didn&rsquo;t encapsulate his devotion to the blues tradition) is contrary to the information in the previous clause (Scott-Heron&rsquo;s resistance to the nickname). </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-671-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$8c3dd402-6b1b-4cd7-85a9-273834df14d5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1ee4485c","external_id":"f60eab77-bd60-4227-a0f7-3a92bc6547e2","vaultid":"6b7b3fce-b1e3-40ce-87d1-e7f54c575452","uId":"b812f2e8-c9ca-49a3-a30c-877a2a872974","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Journalists have dubbed Gil Scott-Heron the &ldquo;godfather of rap,&rdquo; a title that has appeared in hundreds of articles about him since the 1990s. Scott-Heron himself resisted the godfather <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> feeling that it didn&rsquo;t encapsulate his devotion to the broader African American blues music tradition as well as &ldquo;bluesologist,&rdquo; the moniker he preferred. </p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOpti